NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000285 "1;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000286 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000287 "# ABSTRACT: A postmodern object system for Perl 5"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000288 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000289 "__END__"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000290 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000291 "=pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000292 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000293 "=encoding UTF-8"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000294 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000295 "=head1 NAME"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000296 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000297 "Moose - A postmodern object system for Perl 5"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000298 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000299 "=head1 VERSION"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000300 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000301 "version 2.4000"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000302 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000303 "=head1 SYNOPSIS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000304 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000305 "  package Point;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000306 "  use Moose; # automatically turns on strict and warnings"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000307 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000308 "  has 'x' => (is => 'rw', isa => 'Int');"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000309 "  has 'y' => (is => 'rw', isa => 'Int');"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000310 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000311 "  sub clear {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000312 "      my $self = shift;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000313 "      $self->x(0);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000314 "      $self->y(0);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000315 "  }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000316 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000317 "  package Point3D;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000318 "  use Moose;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000319 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000320 "  extends 'Point';"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000321 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000322 "  has 'z' => (is => 'rw', isa => 'Int');"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000323 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000324 "  after 'clear' => sub {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000325 "      my $self = shift;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000326 "      $self->z(0);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000327 "  };"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000328 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000329 "=head1 DESCRIPTION"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000330 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000331 "Moose is an extension of the Perl 5 object system."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000332 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000333 "The main goal of Moose is to make Perl 5 Object Oriented programming"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000334 "easier, more consistent, and less tedious. With Moose you can think"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000335 "more about what you want to do and less about the mechanics of OOP."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000336 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000337 "Additionally, Moose is built on top of L<Class::MOP>, which is a"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000338 "metaclass system for Perl 5. This means that Moose not only makes"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000339 "building normal Perl 5 objects better, but it provides the power of"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000340 "metaclass programming as well."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000341 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000342 "=head2 New to Moose?"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000343 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000344 "If you're new to Moose, the best place to start is the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000345 "L<Moose::Manual> docs, followed by the L<Moose::Cookbook>. The intro"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000346 "will show you what Moose is, and how it makes Perl 5 OO better."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000347 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000348 "The cookbook recipes on Moose basics will get you up to speed with"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000349 "many of Moose's features quickly. Once you have an idea of what Moose"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000350 "can do, you can use the API documentation to get more detail on"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000351 "features which interest you."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000352 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000353 "=head2 Moose Extensions"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000354 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000355 "The C<MooseX::> namespace is the official place to find Moose extensions."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000356 "These extensions can be found on the CPAN.  The easiest way to find them"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000357 "is to search for them (L<https://metacpan.org/search?q=MooseX::>),"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000358 "or to examine L<Task::Moose> which aims to keep an up-to-date, easily"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000359 "installable list of Moose extensions."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000360 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000361 "=head1 TRANSLATIONS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000362 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000363 "Much of the Moose documentation has been translated into other languages."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000364 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000365 "=over 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000366 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000367 "=item Japanese"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000368 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000369 "Japanese docs can be found at"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000370 "L<http://perldoc.perlassociation.org/pod/Moose-Doc-JA/index.html>. The"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000371 "source POD files can be found in GitHub:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000372 "L<http://github.com/jpa/Moose-Doc-JA>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000373 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000374 "=back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000375 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000376 "=head1 BUILDING CLASSES WITH MOOSE"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000377 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000378 "Moose makes every attempt to provide as much convenience as possible during"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000379 "class construction/definition, but still stay out of your way if you want it"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000380 "to. Here are a few items to note when building classes with Moose."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000381 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000382 "When you C<use Moose>, Moose will set the class's parent class to"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000383 "L<Moose::Object>, I<unless> the class using Moose already has a parent"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000384 "class. In addition, specifying a parent with C<extends> will change the parent"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000385 "class."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000386 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000387 "Moose will also manage all attributes (including inherited ones) that are"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000388 "defined with C<has>. And (assuming you call C<new>, which is inherited from"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000389 "L<Moose::Object>) this includes properly initializing all instance slots,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000390 "setting defaults where appropriate, and performing any type constraint checking"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000391 "or coercion."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000392 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000393 "=head1 PROVIDED METHODS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000394 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000395 "Moose provides a number of methods to all your classes, mostly through the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000396 "inheritance of L<Moose::Object>. There is however, one exception. By default,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000397 "Moose will install a method named C<meta> in any class which uses"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000398 "C<Moose>. This method returns the current class's metaclass."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000399 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000400 "If you'd like to rename this method, you can do so by passing the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000401 "C<-meta_name> option when using Moose:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000402 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000403 "    use Moose -meta_name => 'my_meta';"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000404 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000405 "However, the L<Moose::Object> class I<also> provides a method named C<meta>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000406 "which does the same thing. If your class inherits from L<Moose::Object> (which"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000407 "is the default), then you will still have a C<meta> method. However, if your"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000408 "class inherits from a parent which provides a C<meta> method of its own, your"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000409 "class will inherit that instead."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000410 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000411 "If you'd like for Moose to not install a meta method at all, you can pass"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000412 "C<undef> as the C<-meta_name> option:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000413 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000414 "    use Moose -meta_name => undef;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000415 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000416 "Again, you will still inherit C<meta> from L<Moose::Object> in this case."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000417 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000418 "=head1 EXPORTED FUNCTIONS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000419 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000420 "Moose will export a number of functions into the class's namespace which"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000421 "may then be used to set up the class. These functions all work directly"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000422 "on the current class."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000423 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000424 "=head2 extends (@superclasses)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000425 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000426 "This function will set the superclass(es) for the current class. If the parent"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000427 "classes are not yet loaded, then C<extends> tries to load them."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000428 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000429 "This approach is recommended instead of C<use L<base>>/C<use L<parent>>, because"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000430 "C<use base> actually C<push>es onto the class's C<@ISA>, whereas C<extends> will"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000431 "replace it. This is important to ensure that classes which do not have"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000432 "superclasses still properly inherit from L<Moose::Object>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000433 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000434 "Each superclass can be followed by a hash reference with options. Currently,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000435 "only L<-version|Class::MOP/Class Loading Options> is recognized:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000436 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000437 "    extends 'My::Parent'      => { -version => 0.01 },"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000438 "            'My::OtherParent' => { -version => 0.03 };"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000439 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000440 "An exception will be thrown if the version requirements are not"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000441 "satisfied."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000442 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000443 "=head2 with (@roles)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000444 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000445 "This will apply a given set of C<@roles> to the local class."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000446 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000447 "Like with C<extends>, each specified role can be followed by a hash"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000448 "reference with a L<-version|Class::MOP/Class Loading Options> option:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000449 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000450 "    with 'My::Role'      => { -version => 0.32 },"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000451 "         'My::Otherrole' => { -version => 0.23 };"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000452 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000453 "The specified version requirements must be satisfied, otherwise an"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000454 "exception will be thrown."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000455 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000456 "If your role takes options or arguments, they can be passed along in the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000457 "hash reference as well."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000458 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000459 "You should only use one C<with>, even if you are consuming multiple roles. If"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000460 "you consume roles using multiple C<with> statements Moose cannot detect method"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000461 "conflicts between those roles."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000462 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000463 "=head2 has $name|@$names =E<gt> %options"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000464 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000465 "This will install an attribute of a given C<$name> into the current class. If"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000466 "the first parameter is an array reference, it will create an attribute for"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000467 "every C<$name> in the list. The C<%options> will be passed to the constructor"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000468 "for L<Moose::Meta::Attribute> (which inherits from L<Class::MOP::Attribute>),"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000469 "so the full documentation for the valid options can be found there. These are"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000470 "the most commonly used options:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000471 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000472 "=over 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000473 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000474 "=item I<is =E<gt> 'rw'|'ro'>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000475 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000476 "The I<is> option accepts either I<rw> (for read/write) or I<ro> (for read"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000477 "only). These will create either a read/write accessor or a read-only"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000478 "accessor respectively, using the same name as the C<$name> of the attribute."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000479 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000480 "If you need more control over how your accessors are named, you can"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000481 "use the L<reader|Class::MOP::Attribute/reader>,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000482 "L<writer|Class::MOP::Attribute/writer> and"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000483 "L<accessor|Class::MOP::Attribute/accessor> options inherited from"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000484 "L<Class::MOP::Attribute>, however if you use those, you won't need the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000485 "I<is> option."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000486 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000487 "=item I<isa =E<gt> $type_name>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000488 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000489 "The I<isa> option uses Moose's type constraint facilities to set up runtime"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000490 "type checking for this attribute. Moose will perform the checks during class"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000491 "construction, and within any accessors. The C<$type_name> argument must be a"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000492 "string. The string may be either a class name or a type defined using"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000493 "Moose's type definition features. (Refer to L<Moose::Util::TypeConstraints>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000494 "for information on how to define a new type, and how to retrieve type meta-data)."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000495 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000496 "=item I<coerce =E<gt> (1|0)>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000497 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000498 "This will attempt to use coercion with the supplied type constraint to change"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000499 "the value passed into any accessors or constructors. You B<must> supply a type"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000500 "constraint, and that type constraint B<must> define a coercion. See"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000501 "L<Moose::Cookbook::Basics::HTTP_SubtypesAndCoercion> for an example."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000502 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000503 "=item I<does =E<gt> $role_name>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000504 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000505 "This will accept the name of a role which the value stored in this attribute"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000506 "is expected to have consumed."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000507 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000508 "=item I<required =E<gt> (1|0)>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000509 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000510 "This marks the attribute as being required. This means a value must be"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000511 "supplied during class construction, I<or> the attribute must be lazy"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000512 "and have either a default or a builder. Note that C<required> does not"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000513 "say anything about the attribute's value, which can be C<undef>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000514 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000515 "=item I<weak_ref =E<gt> (1|0)>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000516 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000517 "This will tell the class to store the value of this attribute as a weakened"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000518 "reference. If an attribute is a weakened reference, it B<cannot> also be"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000519 "coerced. Note that when a weak ref expires, the attribute's value becomes"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000520 "undefined, and is still considered to be set for purposes of predicate,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000521 "default, etc."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000522 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000523 "=item I<lazy =E<gt> (1|0)>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000524 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000525 "This will tell the class to not create this slot until absolutely necessary."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000526 "If an attribute is marked as lazy it B<must> have a default or builder"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000527 "supplied."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000528 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000529 "=item I<trigger =E<gt> $code>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000530 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000531 "The I<trigger> option is a CODE reference which will be called after"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000532 "the value of the attribute is set. The CODE ref is passed the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000533 "instance itself, the updated value, and the original value if the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000534 "attribute was already set."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000535 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000536 "You B<can> have a trigger on a read-only attribute."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000537 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000538 "B<NOTE:> Triggers will only fire when you B<assign> to the attribute,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000539 "either in the constructor, or using the writer. Default and built values will"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000540 "B<not> cause the trigger to be fired."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000541 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000542 "=item I<handles =E<gt> ARRAY | HASH | REGEXP | ROLE | ROLETYPE | DUCKTYPE | CODE>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000543 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000544 "The I<handles> option provides Moose classes with automated delegation features."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000545 "This is a pretty complex and powerful option. It accepts many different option"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000546 "formats, each with its own benefits and drawbacks."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000547 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000548 "B<NOTE:> The class being delegated to does not need to be a Moose based class,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000549 "which is why this feature is especially useful when wrapping non-Moose classes."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000550 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000551 "All I<handles> option formats share the following traits:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000552 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000553 "You cannot override a locally defined method with a delegated method; an"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000554 "exception will be thrown if you try. That is to say, if you define C<foo> in"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000555 "your class, you cannot override it with a delegated C<foo>. This is almost never"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000556 "something you would want to do, and if it is, you should do it by hand and not"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000557 "use Moose."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000558 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000559 "You cannot override any of the methods found in Moose::Object, or the C<BUILD>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000560 "and C<DEMOLISH> methods. These will not throw an exception, but will silently"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000561 "move on to the next method in the list. My reasoning for this is that you would"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000562 "almost never want to do this, since it usually breaks your class. As with"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000563 "overriding locally defined methods, if you do want to do this, you should do it"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000564 "manually, not with Moose."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000565 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000566 "You do not I<need> to have a reader (or accessor) for the attribute in order"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000567 "to delegate to it. Moose will create a means of accessing the value for you,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000568 "however this will be several times B<less> efficient then if you had given"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000569 "the attribute a reader (or accessor) to use."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000570 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000571 "Below is the documentation for each option format:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000572 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000573 "=over 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000574 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000575 "=item C<ARRAY>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000576 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000577 "This is the most common usage for I<handles>. You basically pass a list of"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000578 "method names to be delegated, and Moose will install a delegation method"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000579 "for each one."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000580 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000581 "=item C<HASH>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000582 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000583 "This is the second most common usage for I<handles>. Instead of a list of"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000584 "method names, you pass a HASH ref where each key is the method name you"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000585 "want installed locally, and its value is the name of the original method"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000586 "in the class being delegated to."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000587 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000588 "This can be very useful for recursive classes like trees. Here is a"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000589 "quick example (soon to be expanded into a Moose::Cookbook recipe):"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000590 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000591 "  package Tree;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000592 "  use Moose;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000593 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000594 "  has 'node' => (is => 'rw', isa => 'Any');"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000595 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000596 "  has 'children' => ("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000597 "      is      => 'ro',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000598 "      isa     => 'ArrayRef',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000599 "      default => sub { [] }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000600 "  );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000601 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000602 "  has 'parent' => ("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000603 "      is          => 'rw',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000604 "      isa         => 'Tree',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000605 "      weak_ref    => 1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000606 "      handles     => {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000607 "          parent_node => 'node',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000608 "          siblings    => 'children',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000609 "      }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000610 "  );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000611 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000612 "In this example, the Tree package gets C<parent_node> and C<siblings> methods,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000613 "which delegate to the C<node> and C<children> methods (respectively) of the Tree"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000614 "instance stored in the C<parent> slot."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000615 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000616 "You may also use an array reference to curry arguments to the original method."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000617 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000618 "  has 'thing' => ("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000619 "      ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000620 "      handles => { set_foo => [ set => 'foo' ] },"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000621 "  );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000622 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000623 "  # $self->set_foo(...) calls $self->thing->set('foo', ...)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000624 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000625 "The first element of the array reference is the original method name, and the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000626 "rest is a list of curried arguments."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000627 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000628 "=item C<REGEXP>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000629 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000630 "The regexp option works very similar to the ARRAY option, except that it builds"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000631 "the list of methods for you. It starts by collecting all possible methods of the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000632 "class being delegated to, then filters that list using the regexp supplied here."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000633 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000634 "B<NOTE:> An I<isa> option is required when using the regexp option format. This"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000635 "is so that we can determine (at compile time) the method list from the class."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000636 "Without an I<isa> this is just not possible."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000637 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000638 "=item C<ROLE> or C<ROLETYPE>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000639 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000640 "With the role option, you specify the name of a role or a"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000641 "L<role type|Moose::Meta::TypeConstraint::Role> whose \"interface\" then becomes"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000642 "the list of methods to handle. The \"interface\" can be defined as; the methods"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000643 "of the role and any required methods of the role. It should be noted that this"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000644 "does B<not> include any method modifiers or generated attribute methods (which"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000645 "is consistent with role composition)."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000646 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000647 "=item C<DUCKTYPE>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000648 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000649 "With the duck type option, you pass a duck type object whose \"interface\" then"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000650 "becomes the list of methods to handle. The \"interface\" can be defined as the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000651 "list of methods passed to C<duck_type> to create a duck type object. For more"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000652 "information on C<duck_type> please check"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000653 "L<Moose::Util::TypeConstraints>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000654 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000655 "=item C<CODE>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000656 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000657 "This is the option to use when you really want to do something funky. You should"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000658 "only use it if you really know what you are doing, as it involves manual"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000659 "metaclass twiddling."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000660 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000661 "This takes a code reference, which should expect two arguments. The first is the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000662 "attribute meta-object this I<handles> is attached to. The second is the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000663 "metaclass of the class being delegated to. It expects you to return a hash (not"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000664 "a HASH ref) of the methods you want mapped."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000665 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000666 "=back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000667 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000668 "=item I<traits =E<gt> [ @role_names ]>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000669 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000670 "This tells Moose to take the list of C<@role_names> and apply them to the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000671 "attribute meta-object. Custom attribute metaclass traits are useful for"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000672 "extending the capabilities of the I<has> keyword: they are the simplest way to"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000673 "extend the MOP, but they are still a fairly advanced topic and too much to"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000674 "cover here."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000675 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000676 "See L<Metaclass and Trait Name Resolution> for details on how a trait name is"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000677 "resolved to a role name."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000678 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000679 "Also see L<Moose::Cookbook::Meta::Labeled_AttributeTrait> for a metaclass"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000680 "trait example."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000681 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000682 "=item I<builder> => Str"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000683 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000684 "The value of this key is the name of the method that will be called to obtain"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000685 "the value used to initialize the attribute. See the L<builder option docs in"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000686 "Class::MOP::Attribute|Class::MOP::Attribute/builder> and/or"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000687 "L<Moose::Cookbook::Basics::BinaryTree_BuilderAndLazyBuild> for more"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000688 "information."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000689 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000690 "=item I<default> => SCALAR | CODE"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000691 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000692 "The value of this key is the default value which will initialize the attribute."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000693 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000694 "NOTE: If the value is a simple scalar (string or number), then it can"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000695 "be just passed as is.  However, if you wish to initialize it with a"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000696 "HASH or ARRAY ref, then you need to wrap that inside a CODE reference."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000697 "See the L<default option docs in"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000698 "Class::MOP::Attribute|Class::MOP::Attribute/default> for more"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000699 "information."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000700 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000701 "=item I<clearer> => Str"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000702 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000703 "Creates a method allowing you to clear the value. See the L<clearer option"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000704 "docs in Class::MOP::Attribute|Class::MOP::Attribute/clearer> for more"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000705 "information."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000706 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000707 "=item I<predicate> => Str"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000708 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000709 "Creates a method to perform a basic test to see if a value has been set in the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000710 "attribute. See the L<predicate option docs in"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000711 "Class::MOP::Attribute|Class::MOP::Attribute/predicate> for more information."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000712 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000713 "Note that the predicate will return true even for a C<weak_ref> attribute"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000714 "whose value has expired."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000715 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000716 "=item I<documentation> => $string"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000717 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000718 "An arbitrary string that can be retrieved later by calling C<<"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000719 "$attr->documentation >>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000720 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000721 "=back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000722 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000723 "=head2 has +$name =E<gt> %options"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000724 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000725 "This is variation on the normal attribute creator C<has> which allows you to"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000726 "clone and extend an attribute from a superclass or from a role. Here is an"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000727 "example of the superclass usage:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000728 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000729 "  package Foo;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000730 "  use Moose;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000731 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000732 "  has 'message' => ("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000733 "      is      => 'rw',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000734 "      isa     => 'Str',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000735 "      default => 'Hello, I am a Foo'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000736 "  );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000737 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000738 "  package My::Foo;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000739 "  use Moose;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000740 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000741 "  extends 'Foo';"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000742 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000743 "  has '+message' => (default => 'Hello I am My::Foo');"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000744 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000745 "What is happening here is that B<My::Foo> is cloning the C<message> attribute"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000746 "from its parent class B<Foo>, retaining the C<is =E<gt> 'rw'> and C<isa =E<gt>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000747 "'Str'> characteristics, but changing the value in C<default>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000748 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000749 "Here is another example, but within the context of a role:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000750 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000751 "  package Foo::Role;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000752 "  use Moose::Role;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000753 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000754 "  has 'message' => ("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000755 "      is      => 'rw',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000756 "      isa     => 'Str',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000757 "      default => 'Hello, I am a Foo'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000758 "  );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000759 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000760 "  package My::Foo;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000761 "  use Moose;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000762 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000763 "  with 'Foo::Role';"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000764 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000765 "  has '+message' => (default => 'Hello I am My::Foo');"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000766 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000767 "In this case, we are basically taking the attribute which the role supplied"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000768 "and altering it within the bounds of this feature."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000769 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000770 "Note that you can only extend an attribute from either a superclass or a role,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000771 "you cannot extend an attribute in a role that composes over an attribute from"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000772 "another role."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000773 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000774 "Aside from where the attributes come from (one from superclass, the other"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000775 "from a role), this feature works exactly the same. This feature is restricted"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000776 "somewhat, so as to try and force at least I<some> sanity into it. Most options work the same, but there are some exceptions:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000777 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000778 "=over 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000779 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000780 "=item I<reader>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000781 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000782 "=item I<writer>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000783 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000784 "=item I<accessor>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000785 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000786 "=item I<clearer>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000787 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000788 "=item I<predicate>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000789 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000790 "These options can be added, but cannot override a superclass definition."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000791 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000792 "=item I<traits>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000793 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000794 "You are allowed to B<add> additional traits to the C<traits> definition."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000795 "These traits will be composed into the attribute, but preexisting traits"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000796 "B<are not> overridden, or removed."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000797 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000798 "=back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000799 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000800 "=head2 before $name|@names|\\@names|qr/.../ =E<gt> sub { ... }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000801 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000802 "=head2 after $name|@names|\\@names|qr/.../ =E<gt> sub { ... }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000803 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000804 "=head2 around $name|@names|\\@names|qr/.../ =E<gt> sub { ... }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000805 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000806 "These three items are syntactic sugar for the before, after, and around method"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000807 "modifier features that L<Class::MOP> provides. More information on these may be"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000808 "found in L<Moose::Manual::MethodModifiers> and the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000809 "L<Class::MOP::Class documentation|Class::MOP::Class/\"Method Modifiers\">."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000810 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000811 "=head2 override ($name, &sub)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000812 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000813 "An C<override> method is a way of explicitly saying \"I am overriding this"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000814 "method from my superclass\". You can call C<super> within this method, and"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000815 "it will work as expected. The same thing I<can> be accomplished with a normal"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000816 "method call and the C<SUPER::> pseudo-package; it is really your choice."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000817 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000818 "=head2 super"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000819 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000820 "The keyword C<super> is a no-op when called outside of an C<override> method. In"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000821 "the context of an C<override> method, it will call the next most appropriate"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000822 "superclass method with the same arguments as the original method."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000823 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000824 "=head2 augment ($name, &sub)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000825 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000826 "An C<augment> method, is a way of explicitly saying \"I am augmenting this"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000827 "method from my superclass\". Once again, the details of how C<inner> and"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000828 "C<augment> work is best described in the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000829 "L<Moose::Cookbook::Basics::Document_AugmentAndInner>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000830 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000831 "=head2 inner"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000832 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000833 "The keyword C<inner>, much like C<super>, is a no-op outside of the context of"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000834 "an C<augment> method. You can think of C<inner> as being the inverse of"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000835 "C<super>; the details of how C<inner> and C<augment> work is best described in"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000836 "the L<Moose::Cookbook::Basics::Document_AugmentAndInner>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000837 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000838 "=head2 blessed"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000839 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000840 "This is the C<Scalar::Util::blessed> function. It is highly recommended that"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000841 "this is used instead of C<ref> anywhere you need to test for an object's class"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000842 "name."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000843 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000844 "=head2 confess"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000845 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000846 "This is the C<Carp::confess> function, and exported here for historical"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000847 "reasons."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000848 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000849 "=head1 METACLASS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000850 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000851 "When you use Moose, you can specify traits which will be applied to your"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000852 "metaclass:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000853 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000854 "    use Moose -traits => 'My::Trait';"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000855 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000856 "This is very similar to the attribute traits feature. When you do"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000857 "this, your class's C<meta> object will have the specified traits"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000858 "applied to it."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000859 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000860 "=head2 Metaclass and Trait Name Resolution"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000861 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000862 "By default, when given a trait name, Moose simply tries to load a"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000863 "class of the same name. If such a class does not exist, it then looks"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000864 "for a class matching"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000865 "B<Moose::Meta::$type::Custom::Trait::$trait_name>. The C<$type>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000866 "variable here will be one of B<Attribute> or B<Class>, depending on"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000867 "what the trait is being applied to."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000868 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000869 "If a class with this long name exists, Moose checks to see if it has"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000870 "the method C<register_implementation>. This method is expected to"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000871 "return the I<real> class name of the trait. If there is no"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000872 "C<register_implementation> method, it will fall back to using"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000873 "B<Moose::Meta::$type::Custom::Trait::$trait> as the trait name."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000874 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000875 "The lookup method for metaclasses is the same, except that it looks"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000876 "for a class matching B<Moose::Meta::$type::Custom::$metaclass_name>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000877 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000878 "If all this is confusing, take a look at"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000879 "L<Moose::Cookbook::Meta::Labeled_AttributeTrait>, which demonstrates how to"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000880 "create an attribute trait."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000881 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000882 "=head1 UNIMPORTING FUNCTIONS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000883 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000884 "=head2 B<unimport>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000885 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000886 "Moose offers a way to remove the keywords it exports, through the C<unimport>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000887 "method. You simply have to say C<no Moose> at the bottom of your code for this"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000888 "to work. Here is an example:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000889 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000890 "    package Person;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000891 "    use Moose;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000892 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000893 "    has 'first_name' => (is => 'rw', isa => 'Str');"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000894 "    has 'last_name'  => (is => 'rw', isa => 'Str');"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000895 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000896 "    sub full_name {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000897 "        my $self = shift;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000898 "        $self->first_name . ' ' . $self->last_name"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000899 "    }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000900 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000901 "    no Moose; # keywords are removed from the Person package"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000902 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000903 "=head1 EXTENDING AND EMBEDDING MOOSE"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000904 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000905 "To learn more about extending Moose, we recommend checking out the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000906 "\"Extending\" recipes in the L<Moose::Cookbook>, starting with"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000907 "L<Moose::Cookbook::Extending::ExtensionOverview>, which provides an overview of"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000908 "all the different ways you might extend Moose. L<Moose::Exporter> and"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000909 "L<Moose::Util::MetaRole> are the modules which provide the majority of the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000910 "extension functionality, so reading their documentation should also be helpful."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000911 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000912 "=head2 The MooseX:: namespace"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000913 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000914 "Generally if you're writing an extension I<for> Moose itself you'll want"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000915 "to put your extension in the C<MooseX::> namespace. This namespace is"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000916 "specifically for extensions that make Moose better or different in some"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000917 "fundamental way. It is traditionally B<not> for a package that just happens"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000918 "to use Moose. This namespace follows from the examples of the C<LWPx::>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000919 "and C<DBIx::> namespaces that perform the same function for C<LWP> and C<DBI>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000920 "respectively."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000921 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000922 "=head1 METACLASS COMPATIBILITY AND MOOSE"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000923 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000924 "Metaclass compatibility is a thorny subject. You should start by"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000925 "reading the \"About Metaclass compatibility\" section in the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000926 "L<Class::MOP> docs."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000927 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000928 "Moose will attempt to resolve a few cases of metaclass incompatibility"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000929 "when you set the superclasses for a class, in addition to the cases that"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000930 "L<Class::MOP> handles."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000931 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000932 "Moose tries to determine if the metaclasses only \"differ by roles\". This"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000933 "means that the parent and child's metaclass share a common ancestor in"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000934 "their respective hierarchies, and that the subclasses under the common"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000935 "ancestor are only different because of role applications. This case is"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000936 "actually fairly common when you mix and match various C<MooseX::*>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000937 "modules, many of which apply roles to the metaclass."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000938 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000939 "If the parent and child do differ by roles, Moose replaces the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000940 "metaclass in the child with a newly created metaclass. This metaclass"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000941 "is a subclass of the parent's metaclass which does all of the roles that"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000942 "the child's metaclass did before being replaced. Effectively, this"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000943 "means the new metaclass does all of the roles done by both the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000944 "parent's and child's original metaclasses."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000945 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000946 "Ultimately, this is all transparent to you except in the case of an"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000947 "unresolvable conflict."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000948 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000949 "=head1 CAVEATS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000950 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000951 "It should be noted that C<super> and C<inner> B<cannot> be used in the same"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000952 "method. However, they may be combined within the same class hierarchy; see"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000953 "F<t/basics/override_augment_inner_super.t> for an example."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000954 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000955 "The reason for this is that C<super> is only valid within a method"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000956 "with the C<override> modifier, and C<inner> will never be valid within an"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000957 "C<override> method. In fact, C<augment> will skip over any C<override> methods"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000958 "when searching for its appropriate C<inner>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000959 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000960 "This might seem like a restriction, but I am of the opinion that keeping these"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000961 "two features separate (yet interoperable) actually makes them easy to use, since"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000962 "their behavior is then easier to predict. Time will tell whether I am right or"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000963 "not (UPDATE: so far so good)."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000964 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000965 "=head1 GETTING HELP"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000966 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000967 "We offer both a mailing list and a very active IRC channel."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000968 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000969 "The mailing list is L<mailto:moose@perl.org>. You must be subscribed to send"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000970 "a message. To subscribe, send an empty message to"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000971 "L<mailto:moose-subscribe@perl.org>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000972 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000973 "You can also visit us at C<#moose> on L<irc://irc.perl.org/#moose>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000974 "This channel is quite active, and questions at all levels (on Moose-related"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000975 "topics ;) are welcome."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000976 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000977 "=head1 WHAT DOES MOOSE STAND FOR?"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000978 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000979 "Moose doesn't stand for one thing in particular, however, if you want, here"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000980 "are a few of our favorites. Feel free to contribute more!"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000981 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000982 "=over 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000983 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000984 "=item * Make Other Object Systems Envious"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000985 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000986 "=item * Makes Object Orientation So Easy"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000987 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000988 "=item * Makes Object Orientation Spiffy- Er (sorry ingy)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000989 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000990 "=item * Most Other Object Systems Emasculate"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000991 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000992 "=item * Moose Often Ovulate Sorta Early"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000993 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000994 "=item * Moose Offers Often Super Extensions"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000995 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000996 "=item * Meta Object Obligates Salivary Excitation"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000997 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000998 "=item * Meta Object Orientation Syntax Extensions"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000999 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001000 "=item * Moo, Only Overengineered, Slow, and Execrable (blame rjbs!)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001001 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001002 "=item * Massive Object-Oriented Stacktrace Emitter"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001003 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001004 "=back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001005 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001006 "=head1 ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001007 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001008 "=over 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001009 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001010 "=item I blame Sam Vilain for introducing me to the insanity that is meta-models."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001011 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001012 "=item I blame Audrey Tang for then encouraging my meta-model habit in #perl6."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001013 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001014 "=item Without Yuval \"nothingmuch\" Kogman this module would not be possible,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001015 "and it certainly wouldn't have this name ;P"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001016 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001017 "=item The basis of the TypeContraints module was Rob Kinyon's idea"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001018 "originally, I just ran with it."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001019 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001020 "=item Thanks to mst & chansen and the whole #moose posse for all the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001021 "early ideas/feature-requests/encouragement/bug-finding."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001022 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001023 "=item Thanks to David \"Theory\" Wheeler for meta-discussions and spelling fixes."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001024 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001025 "=back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001026 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001027 "=head1 SEE ALSO"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001028 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001029 "=over 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001030 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001031 "=item L<http://moose.perl.org/>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001032 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001033 "This is the official web home of Moose. It contains links to our public git"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001034 "repository, as well as links to a number of talks and articles on Moose and"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001035 "Moose related technologies."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001036 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001037 "=item the L<Moose manual|Moose::Manual>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001038 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001039 "This is an introduction to Moose which covers most of the basics."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001040 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001041 "=item Modern Perl, by chromatic"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001042 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001043 "This is an introduction to modern Perl programming, which includes a section on"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001044 "Moose. It is available in print and as a free download from"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001045 "L<http://onyxneon.com/books/modern_perl/>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001046 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001047 "=item The Moose is flying, a tutorial by Randal Schwartz"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001048 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001049 "Part 1 - L<http://www.stonehenge.com/merlyn/LinuxMag/col94.html>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001050 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001051 "Part 2 - L<http://www.stonehenge.com/merlyn/LinuxMag/col95.html>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001052 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001053 "=item Several Moose extension modules in the C<MooseX::> namespace."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001054 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001055 "See L<https://metacpan.org/search?q=MooseX::> for extensions."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001056 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001057 "=back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001058 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001059 "=head2 Books"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001060 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001061 "=over 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001062 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001063 "=item The Art of the MetaObject Protocol"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001064 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001065 "I mention this in the L<Class::MOP> docs too, as this book was critical in"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001066 "the development of both modules and is highly recommended."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001067 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001068 "=back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001069 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001070 "=head2 Papers"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001071 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001072 "=over 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001073 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001074 "=item L<http://www.cs.utah.edu/plt/publications/oopsla04-gff.pdf>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001075 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001076 "This paper (suggested by lbr on #moose) was what lead to the implementation"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001077 "of the C<super>/C<override> and C<inner>/C<augment> features. If you really"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001078 "want to understand them, I suggest you read this."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001079 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001080 "=back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001081 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001082 "=head1 BUGS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001083 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001084 "All complex software has bugs lurking in it, and this module is no"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001085 "exception."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001086 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001087 "Please report any bugs to C<bug-moose@rt.cpan.org>, or through the web"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001088 "interface at L<http://rt.cpan.org>. You can also submit a C<TODO> test as a"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001089 "pull request at L<https://github.com/moose/Moose>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001090 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001091 "You can also discuss feature requests or possible bugs on the Moose mailing"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001092 "list (moose@perl.org) or on IRC at L<irc://irc.perl.org/#moose>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001093 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001094 "=head1 FEATURE REQUESTS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001095 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001096 "We are very strict about what features we add to the Moose core, especially"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001097 "the user-visible features. Instead we have made sure that the underlying"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001098 "meta-system of Moose is as extensible as possible so that you can add your"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001099 "own features easily."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001100 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001101 "That said, occasionally there is a feature needed in the meta-system"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001102 "to support your planned extension, in which case you should either"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001103 "email the mailing list (moose@perl.org) or join us on IRC at"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001104 "L<irc://irc.perl.org/#moose> to discuss. The"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001105 "L<Moose::Manual::Contributing> has more detail about how and when you"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001106 "can contribute."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001107 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001108 "=head1 CABAL"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001109 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001110 "There are only a few people with the rights to release a new version"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001111 "of Moose. The Moose Cabal are the people to go to with questions regarding"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001112 "the wider purview of Moose. They help maintain not just the code"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001113 "but the community as well. See the list below under L</AUTHORS>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001114 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001115 "=head1 CONTRIBUTORS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001116 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001117 "Moose is a community project, and as such, involves the work of many, many"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001118 "members of the community beyond just the members in the cabal. In particular:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001119 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001120 "Dave (autarch) Rolsky wrote most of the documentation in L<Moose::Manual>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001121 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001122 "John (jgoulah) Goulah wrote L<Moose::Cookbook::Snack::Keywords>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001123 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001124 "Jess (castaway) Robinson wrote L<Moose::Cookbook::Snack::Types>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001125 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001126 "Aran (bluefeet) Clary Deltac wrote"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001127 "L<Moose::Cookbook::Basics::Genome_OverloadingSubtypesAndCoercion>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001128 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001129 "Anders (Debolaz) Nor Berle contributed L<Test::Moose> and L<Moose::Util>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001130 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001131 "Also, the code in L<Moose::Meta::Attribute::Native> is based on code from the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001132 "L<MooseX::AttributeHelpers> distribution, which had contributions from:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001133 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001134 "Chris (perigrin) Prather"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001135 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001136 "Cory (gphat) Watson"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001137 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001138 "Evan Carroll"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001139 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001140 "Florian (rafl) Ragwitz"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001141 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001142 "Jason May"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001143 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001144 "Jay Hannah"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001145 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001146 "Jesse (doy) Luehrs"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001147 ""
